WEBVTT
NOTE why-are-deep-sea-creatures-so-big - timed from measured narration audio

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.656
A giant isopod is only giant beside the
woodlice under a garden stone.

00:00:04.656 --> 00:00:07.211
Most deep-sea creatures are not big.

00:00:07.211 --> 00:00:12.535
Gigantism appears in particular groups,
mainly cold-blooded crustaceans and

00:00:12.535 --> 00:00:17.077
molluscs such as giant isopods, giant
amphipods and giant squid.

00:00:17.077 --> 00:00:22.398
For those groups, the sharpest single clue
came in 1999, when a study in Nature

00:00:22.398 --> 00:00:27.045
linked the largest amphipod sizes to how
much oxygen the water holds.

00:00:27.045 --> 00:00:31.296
That settles less than it sounds. It shows
where the giants tend to occur.

00:00:31.296 --> 00:00:36.783
On its own, it does not show why they
grow, or whether depth has anything to do

00:00:36.783 --> 00:00:37.339
with it.

00:00:37.339 --> 00:00:39.755
Why are deep sea creatures so big?

00:00:39.755 --> 00:00:42.500
Start with the part the popular image
leaves out.

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:46.982
Watch footage from a remotely operated
vehicle descending through the deep water

00:00:46.982 --> 00:00:51.689
column, and you will see that most of what
drifts past its lights is small: jellies,

00:00:51.689 --> 00:00:55.947
shrimp, copepods, worms, and the larvae of
animals that live somewhere else.

00:00:55.947 --> 00:01:00.348
On the seafloor, much of the life is
buried in the mud, and a lot of it is too

00:01:00.348 --> 00:01:02.267
small to see without a microscope.

00:01:02.267 --> 00:01:05.707
The giants in the thumbnails are real, but
they are scattered.

00:01:05.707 --> 00:01:09.536
Animals you could hold on a fingertip
outnumber them many times over.

00:01:09.536 --> 00:01:12.543
So the honest version of the question is
narrower.

00:01:12.543 --> 00:01:17.413
Why do some deep-sea animals grow much
larger than their shallow-water relatives?

00:01:17.413 --> 00:01:22.864
Biologists call that pattern deep-sea
gigantism, or sometimes abyssal gigantism.

00:01:22.864 --> 00:01:28.043
You will find the term in explainers
published by NOAA Ocean Exploration and

00:01:28.043 --> 00:01:29.269
Smithsonian Ocean.

00:01:29.269 --> 00:01:31.571
The word relatives is doing the work.

00:01:31.571 --> 00:01:36.610
A giant isopod is only giant next to the
small isopods of coastlines and gardens,

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:39.472
the woodlice you may have found under a
stone.

00:01:39.472 --> 00:01:43.573
It is always a comparison within a group,
not across the whole ocean.

00:01:43.573 --> 00:01:47.280
The examples that come up again and again
have something in common.

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:52.012
The giant isopod, Bathynomus giganteus,
scavenges on the deep seafloor.

00:01:52.012 --> 00:01:57.276
Alicella gigantea is a giant amphipod, a
relative of the small sandhoppers on a

00:01:57.276 --> 00:02:01.675
beach, and it has been hauled up in baited
traps from great depth.

00:02:01.675 --> 00:02:05.705
Sea spiders are usually tiny, but in cold
water they reach the size of a dinner

00:02:05.705 --> 00:02:06.011
plate.

00:02:06.011 --> 00:02:11.012
The giant squid, Architeuthis, is among
the longest invertebrates known.

00:02:11.012 --> 00:02:15.040
Fish, as a group, do not show the same
consistent pattern.

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:20.140
Many of the most common deep-sea fish,
such as bristlemouths and lanternfish, are

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:20.517
small.

00:02:20.517 --> 00:02:22.144
Look at what the giants share.

00:02:22.144 --> 00:02:26.592
They are ectotherms, animals whose body
temperature follows the water around them.

00:02:26.592 --> 00:02:28.632
Most are crustaceans or molluscs.

00:02:28.632 --> 00:02:33.579
The same groups also produce giants in the
shallow, cold seas around Antarctica,

00:02:33.579 --> 00:02:35.557
where great depth plays no part.

00:02:35.557 --> 00:02:39.346
That overlap is the first real clue in
this story.

00:02:39.346 --> 00:02:43.211
It suggests that depth itself may not be
the cause.

00:02:43.211 --> 00:02:43.979
Cold may be.

00:02:43.979 --> 00:02:49.232
On this reading, depth matters mostly
because the deep is where the cold water

00:02:49.232 --> 00:02:53.184
We talk about the ocean like it's a known
thing because it's on every map.

00:02:53.184 --> 00:02:56.344
Most of what's under the surface is barely
documented.

00:02:56.344 --> 00:02:59.621
We're living on a planet we haven't
finished looking at.

00:02:59.621 --> 00:03:04.254
Biologists have long observed a pattern
called Bergmann's rule: within related

00:03:04.254 --> 00:03:07.520
groups, larger forms tend to be found in
colder places.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:10.364
It was first described for warm-blooded
animals.

00:03:10.364 --> 00:03:14.393
A related pattern holds for ectotherms,
and it is usually called the

00:03:14.393 --> 00:03:15.696
temperature-size rule.

00:03:15.696 --> 00:03:21.287
Raise the same species in colder water in
a laboratory and, very often, it grows

00:03:21.287 --> 00:03:25.131
more slowly but reaches maturity at a
larger body size.

00:03:25.131 --> 00:03:30.515
In many of these animals, the cells
themselves also tend to be larger at low

00:03:30.515 --> 00:03:31.365
temperature.

00:03:31.365 --> 00:03:36.553
Below the surface layers, the deep sea is
cold, close to freezing in many places,

00:03:36.553 --> 00:03:38.539
and it stays that way all year.

00:03:38.539 --> 00:03:40.896
No warm season speeds things up.

00:03:40.896 --> 00:03:46.640
So the chain runs like this: cold water,
slower growth, later maturity, larger

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:47.451
adult body.

00:03:47.451 --> 00:03:50.917
The way this mechanism is known is what
gives it weight.

00:03:50.917 --> 00:03:54.197
You do not have to infer it from a few odd
specimens.

00:03:54.197 --> 00:03:59.334
You can test it in tanks, changing one
variable at a time and holding the rest

00:03:59.334 --> 00:04:02.693
steady, and the result repeats across many
species.

00:04:02.693 --> 00:04:06.997
Temperature alone does not explain why
size should have an upper limit.

00:04:06.997 --> 00:04:12.155
Oxygen might. As an animal grows, its
volume rises faster than its surface.

00:04:12.155 --> 00:04:17.484
Larger animals therefore have less surface
relative to their volume, and getting

00:04:17.484 --> 00:04:19.883
oxygen to every cell becomes harder.

00:04:19.883 --> 00:04:22.333
Cold water helps in two separate ways.

00:04:22.333 --> 00:04:26.653
First, cold water holds more dissolved
oxygen than warm water does.

00:04:26.653 --> 00:04:31.850
Second, cold slows the animal's
metabolism, so each cell needs less.

00:04:31.850 --> 00:04:34.755
Supply goes up while demand goes down.

00:04:34.755 --> 00:04:40.153
In 1999, Gauthier Chapelle and Lloyd Peck,
the latter of the British Antarctic

00:04:40.153 --> 00:04:43.891
Survey, published the amphipod oxygen
study in Nature.

00:04:43.891 --> 00:04:45.634
Amphipods were a good choice.

00:04:45.634 --> 00:04:50.622
They live almost everywhere, in warm water
and in cold, and the largest known sizes

00:04:50.622 --> 00:04:53.147
of many species had already been recorded.

00:04:53.147 --> 00:04:57.880
The two researchers compared those maximum
sizes against the conditions where the

00:04:57.880 --> 00:04:58.699
animals lived.

00:04:58.699 --> 00:05:01.870
Temperature on its own was not the best
predictor.

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:04.850
The amount of oxygen the water could carry
was.

00:05:04.850 --> 00:05:08.592
Where oxygen was plentiful, the biggest
amphipods appeared.

00:05:08.592 --> 00:05:10.745
Why would that apply to the deep sea?

00:05:10.745 --> 00:05:15.051
Much of the ocean's deep water began as
cold surface water near the poles.

00:05:15.051 --> 00:05:20.016
That water sank and spread along the ocean
floor, carrying its oxygen with it.

00:05:20.016 --> 00:05:25.314
So a great deal of the deep sea is both
cold and reasonably well oxygenated, the

00:05:25.314 --> 00:05:28.096
combination the amphipod study pointed to.

00:05:28.096 --> 00:05:29.943
There is a useful exception.

00:05:29.943 --> 00:05:35.220
In many regions, layers at middle depths
run low on oxygen, and those layers are

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:39.376
not known for giants. That fits the idea.
It does not prove it.

00:05:39.376 --> 00:05:42.501
The second family of explanations is about
food.

00:05:42.501 --> 00:05:46.603
Below the sunlit layer, nothing
photosynthesises in open water.

00:05:46.603 --> 00:05:51.339
Most deep-sea life therefore depends on
what sinks from above: dead plankton,

00:05:51.339 --> 00:05:56.505
faecal pellets, flakes of drifting debris,
and now and then the carcass of a fish or

00:05:56.505 --> 00:06:00.933
a whale. Food arrives rarely, and it lands
in places nobody can predict.

00:06:00.933 --> 00:06:04.009
A larger body changes the arithmetic. It
stores more.

00:06:04.009 --> 00:06:08.362
And under a relationship known as
Kleiber's law, bigger animals burn energy

00:06:08.362 --> 00:06:11.264
more slowly per gram of tissue than small
ones do.

00:06:11.264 --> 00:06:12.572
Put those together.

00:06:12.572 --> 00:06:18.009
Food arrives rarely, a larger body stores
more, it burns energy more slowly per

00:06:18.009 --> 00:06:21.381
gram, and so a big scavenger can travel
and wait.

00:06:21.381 --> 00:06:26.129
Giant isopods kept in aquariums are known
for going very long stretches without

00:06:26.129 --> 00:06:26.549
eating.

00:06:26.549 --> 00:06:31.879
A large amphipod can cover more seafloor
and reach a fallen fish before smaller

00:06:31.879 --> 00:06:32.891
competitors do.

00:06:32.891 --> 00:06:36.177
This explanation has a weakness worth
saying aloud.

00:06:36.177 --> 00:06:38.496
Scarce food can argue the other way.

00:06:38.496 --> 00:06:41.569
Less food can just as easily mean smaller
bodies.

00:06:41.569 --> 00:06:46.523
In many deep-sea groups, typical size
falls with depth, while in some it rises.

00:06:46.523 --> 00:06:50.831
Food may favour giants among mobile
scavengers and favour small bodies among

00:06:50.831 --> 00:06:52.928
animals that sit in the mud and wait.

00:06:52.928 --> 00:06:56.210
Scarce food is not a push toward large
size everywhere.

00:06:56.210 --> 00:07:00.864
The same pressure produces different
outcomes depending on how an animal makes

00:07:00.864 --> 00:07:01.520
its living.

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:06.176
There is also a quieter problem underneath
all the explanations so far: how the

00:07:06.176 --> 00:07:08.416
animals were found in the first place.

00:07:08.416 --> 00:07:12.990
Much of what is known about deep-sea
giants comes from trawls dragged along the

00:07:12.990 --> 00:07:16.059
seafloor and from baited traps lowered on
long lines.

00:07:16.059 --> 00:07:20.962
A baited trap attracts mobile scavengers,
which are exactly the animals the food

00:07:20.962 --> 00:07:22.923
argument predicts will be large.

00:07:22.923 --> 00:07:27.077
A coarse net keeps big animals and lets
small ones slip through the mesh.

00:07:27.077 --> 00:07:31.871
And big, strange specimens are the ones
that get described, photographed and

00:07:31.871 --> 00:07:32.565
remembered.

00:07:32.565 --> 00:07:35.451
None of this makes the giants imaginary.

00:07:35.451 --> 00:07:39.419
A giant isopod on a laboratory bench is a
giant isopod.

00:07:39.419 --> 00:07:44.001
But the impression of a deep sea full of
large animals partly reflects what the

00:07:44.001 --> 00:07:45.973
trap caught, not what lives there.

00:07:45.973 --> 00:07:50.641
Fair comparisons need the same gear, used
the same way, at the same depths, across

00:07:50.641 --> 00:07:54.512
many sites, with shallow-water relatives
measured just as carefully.

00:07:54.512 --> 00:07:59.111
Studies built like that are slow and
expensive, and there are still relatively

00:07:59.111 --> 00:07:59.819
few of them.

00:07:59.819 --> 00:08:04.708
For most of history, the largest deep-sea
animals were known only from dead or dying

00:08:04.708 --> 00:08:07.909
specimens, washed ashore or brought up
tangled in nets.

00:08:07.909 --> 00:08:11.765
That limits what you can learn. You can
measure a body on a deck.

00:08:11.765 --> 00:08:16.864
You cannot see how it hunts, how fast it
moves, or how much energy it spends doing

00:08:16.864 --> 00:08:17.051
so.

00:08:17.051 --> 00:08:22.891
In 2004, Tsunemi Kubodera and a colleague
took the first photographs of a live giant

00:08:22.891 --> 00:08:24.907
squid in the wild, off Japan.

00:08:24.907 --> 00:08:31.036
In 2012, a team including Kubodera
recorded the first video of a giant squid

00:08:31.036 --> 00:08:32.891
in its natural habitat.

00:08:32.891 --> 00:08:38.170
In 2025, an expedition run by the Schmidt
Ocean Institute with the Ocean Census

00:08:38.170 --> 00:08:43.316
filmed a colossal squid alive in its
natural habitat for the first time, near

00:08:43.316 --> 00:08:45.120
the South Sandwich Islands.

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:49.072
It was a juvenile, and it was small. That
is a useful correction.

00:08:49.072 --> 00:08:54.291
Even the animal often described as the
heaviest invertebrate on Earth starts life

00:08:54.291 --> 00:08:54.613
tiny.

00:08:54.613 --> 00:08:59.180
How it becomes enormous is a question
about growth in cold, dark water over a

00:08:59.180 --> 00:09:03.568
long time, and live observation can begin
to answer that kind of question.

00:09:03.568 --> 00:09:07.124
When footage of a deep-sea giant appears,
watch for scale.

00:09:07.124 --> 00:09:12.028
Many remotely operated vehicles project
laser dots a fixed distance apart, which

00:09:12.028 --> 00:09:14.603
lets scientists measure what is on screen.

00:09:14.603 --> 00:09:19.941
Without a scale bar or laser dots, size on
video is a guess from the frame.

00:09:19.941 --> 00:09:22.010
Notice, too, where the animal is.

00:09:22.010 --> 00:09:26.775
Often it is at bait, or in a vehicle's
lights, which is the sampling problem

00:09:26.775 --> 00:09:27.904
described earlier.

00:09:27.904 --> 00:09:32.592
And notice what is not on screen: the
small animals all around it.

00:09:32.592 --> 00:09:35.844
Here is what this evidence does not
establish.

00:09:35.844 --> 00:09:39.733
It does not show that depth itself makes
animals large.

00:09:39.733 --> 00:09:44.893
Cold, oxygen and food all change with
depth, and they are hard to separate in

00:09:44.893 --> 00:09:45.563
the field.

00:09:45.563 --> 00:09:50.631
The oxygen link is a correlation across
species, strongest in amphipods, and

00:09:50.631 --> 00:09:54.699
researchers still debate how well it
extends to other groups.

00:09:54.699 --> 00:09:57.409
So whether oxygen sets the ceiling remains
open.

00:09:57.409 --> 00:10:01.926
Which cause matters most probably differs
from one group to another, and in many

00:10:01.926 --> 00:10:03.845
groups it has never been measured.

00:10:03.845 --> 00:10:05.649
What is settled is narrower.

00:10:05.649 --> 00:10:10.866
Gigantism appears in particular groups,
and cold water slows growth in ectotherms

00:10:10.866 --> 00:10:12.992
in ways laboratories can measure.

00:10:12.992 --> 00:10:16.597
The deep sea is not a place where
everything grows huge.

00:10:16.597 --> 00:10:21.941
It is a place where a few kinds of animal,
in cold water, given time, and with food

00:10:21.941 --> 00:10:26.512
scattered far apart, can outgrow their
relatives in warm, shallow seas.

00:10:26.512 --> 00:10:28.042
The giants are real.

00:10:28.042 --> 00:10:34.009
The explanation is still being assembled,
one trap, one camera and one careful

00:10:34.009 --> 00:10:35.616
comparison at a time.
